999
00:48:29.490 --> 00:48:30.350
She would go in.

1000
00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:31.770
She'd be like, I got it.

1001
00:48:31.770 --> 00:48:37.090
You want to recreate season five, episode 13, scene 4A?

1002
00:48:37.350 --> 00:48:40.610
And she'd flip to the page and there all of our hair would be.

1003
00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:44.030
And she would be like, Angela, you were half up, half down.

1004
00:48:44.430 --> 00:48:46.110
Jenna, you were in a ponytail.

1005
00:48:47.170 --> 00:48:52.930
Lady, part of my digital clutter and photos from this episode, I took a lot of our crew.

1006
00:48:53.390 --> 00:48:54.290
You did?

1007
00:48:54.290 --> 00:48:54.750
Yeah.

1008
00:48:54.950 --> 00:48:58.790
So I have hair and makeup backstage, you know, at their tables.

1009
00:48:58.930 --> 00:49:03.730
And I have our crew all in the warehouse as we were figuring out the paper airplane scenes.

1010
00:49:03.950 --> 00:49:05.630
I'll put all of them in stories.

1011
00:49:05.810 --> 00:49:08.390
It just, I just love seeing everyone.

1012
00:49:08.990 --> 00:49:12.990
Well, speaking of the warehouse, the competition is underway.

1013
00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:15.610
And it's going to start with Kevin versus Angela.

1014
00:49:16.190 --> 00:49:20.790
Yeah, Kevin has a pretty fancy looking plane and Dwight questions it.

1015
00:49:20.910 --> 00:49:21.990
He's looking out for Angela.

1016
00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:24.350
He's like, you made that paper airplane?

1017
00:49:24.670 --> 00:49:28.270
And Brian's performance is so great right here.

1018
00:49:28.450 --> 00:49:31.250
Like he literally, it's like he manifests a sweat.

1019
00:49:31.890 --> 00:49:34.670
Kevin's like, well, sort of.

1020
00:49:34.830 --> 00:49:37.390
And then we find out that Kevin bought it online.

1021
00:49:38.030 --> 00:49:38.390
Yeah.

1022
00:49:38.910 --> 00:49:41.750
So they call for a refold.

1023
00:49:42.150 --> 00:49:43.750
He has to refold.

1024
00:49:43.870 --> 00:49:46.050
And you're going to see him at like a table.

1025
00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:52.390
And he's been folding planes forever, trying to make a plane.

1026
00:49:52.990 --> 00:49:53.970
Guess what?

1027
00:49:55.070 --> 00:49:56.790
Facts coming your way.

1028
00:49:57.250 --> 00:49:58.330
Paper airplane facts.

1029
00:49:58.330 --> 00:49:59.970
Facts inspired by this moment.

1030
00:50:00.350 --> 00:50:04.910
Do you know what the most paper airplanes made in one hour is?

1031
00:50:05.250 --> 00:50:06.630
In one hour?

1032
00:50:07.390 --> 00:50:15.110
Someone made 12,026 paper airplanes, which is about one every 3.3 seconds.

1033
00:50:15.410 --> 00:50:22.550
Do you think the person that did that has like little special like bandages on their fingers?

1034
00:50:22.550 --> 00:50:27.050
Like how my mom has that little rubber thing you put on your finger when you're sewing?

1035
00:50:27.390 --> 00:50:28.110
You muSt.

1036
00:50:28.330 --> 00:50:33.310
Because the paper cuts, I feel like, are all over the place.

1037
00:50:33.610 --> 00:50:33.810
Yeah.

1038
00:50:34.670 --> 00:50:40.890
Also, I mean, what is the quality of a paper airplane that you made in 3.3 seconds?

1039
00:50:41.190 --> 00:50:41.590
I don't know.

1040
00:50:41.750 --> 00:50:43.130
There's no quality control here.

1041
00:50:43.290 --> 00:50:43.370
There's no quality.

1042
00:50:43.370 --> 00:50:44.390
It's just volume.

1043
00:50:45.070 --> 00:50:45.370
Mm-hmm.

1044
00:50:45.490 --> 00:50:51.850
I also found out that the fastest time to fold and then throw a paper airplane.

1045
00:50:51.850 --> 00:50:56.730
So in this case, you actually had to make like a flyable device.

1046
00:50:56.730 --> 00:51:03.350
So fold and throw, the fastest time is 7.03 seconds.

1047
00:51:04.130 --> 00:51:06.170
Seven seconds?

1048
00:51:06.610 --> 00:51:07.130
Yeah.

1049
00:51:07.350 --> 00:51:08.590
7.03.

1050
00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:11.310
You would fold and then throw it and it flew.

1051
00:51:11.830 --> 00:51:14.210
That is the fastest time.

1052
00:51:14.610 --> 00:51:15.410
That's crazy.

1053
00:51:15.410 --> 00:51:27.850
And so that's why I'm like, okay, if the most paper airplanes made in one hour was like a little over 12,000, they're having to fold like these paper airplanes in 3.3 seconds.

1054
00:51:27.850 --> 00:51:32.470
But in order to fold and throw one that flies, the fastest is 7 seconds.

1055
00:51:32.470 --> 00:51:37.030
I'm thinking a lot of those 12,000 airplanes were not flight worthy.

1056
00:51:37.550 --> 00:51:38.530
You know what I'm saying?

1057
00:51:38.970 --> 00:51:39.470
You think?

1058
00:51:41.150 --> 00:51:50.170
Similar to Kevin's pile, which looks like a bunch of just randomly folded paper and maybe 20% look like airplanes.

1059
00:51:50.430 --> 00:51:51.070
I don't know.

1060
00:51:51.070 --> 00:51:51.530
Yeah.

1061
00:51:51.830 --> 00:51:52.510
He's struggling.

1062
00:51:53.050 --> 00:51:58.990
I asked Warren and Halstead if there were any deleted scenes that they wish could have made it in this episode.

1063
00:51:59.150 --> 00:52:01.430
And Halstead shared a talking head that they wrote.

1064
00:52:01.610 --> 00:52:02.790
It's in the shooting draft.

1065
00:52:03.410 --> 00:52:04.850
And it is really funny.

1066
00:52:05.010 --> 00:52:05.630
I'm going to read it.

1067
00:52:05.970 --> 00:52:07.450
It's Dwight.

1068
00:52:07.630 --> 00:52:12.050
And he's just called Kevin out that Kevin did not make that paper airplane.

1069
00:52:12.750 --> 00:52:14.610
And here was a talking head.

1070
00:52:14.990 --> 00:52:15.890
Dwight says,

1071
00:52:16.030 --> 00:52:18.310
There is no way he built that plane.

1072
00:52:18.830 --> 00:52:23.790
I once watched him spend 20 minutes trying to put an envelope into a letter.

1073
00:52:24.070 --> 00:52:24.910
You heard me.

1074
00:52:25.470 --> 00:52:26.670
You heard me.

1075
00:52:28.190 --> 00:52:29.850
Anyway, that cracked me up, too.

1076
00:52:30.110 --> 00:52:30.330
That's pretty great.

1077
00:52:30.730 --> 00:52:35.310
Well, something we should mention is that Angela has taken notice of Dwight's behavior here.

1078
00:52:35.670 --> 00:52:36.170
Yeah.

1079
00:52:36.210 --> 00:52:37.250
She has a talking head.

1080
00:52:37.530 --> 00:52:38.250
She does.

1081
00:52:38.250 --> 00:52:39.330
She says to camera,

1082
00:52:39.570 --> 00:52:40.870
Was Dwight rooting for me?

1083
00:52:41.010 --> 00:52:41.250
Hmm.

1084
00:52:41.410 --> 00:52:42.410
I hadn't noticed.

1085
00:52:42.930 --> 00:52:43.310
Mm-hmm.

1086
00:52:43.770 --> 00:52:44.070
Yeah.

1087
00:52:44.590 --> 00:52:47.010
She's very delighted by this.

1088
00:52:47.010 --> 00:52:48.330
She sure is.

1089
00:52:48.550 --> 00:52:54.770
Back in the bullpen, Jim has brought Pam a cup of tea, which she appreciates.

1090
00:52:55.030 --> 00:52:55.790
She does.

1091
00:52:56.430 --> 00:53:03.950
He shares that the truth is he was going in the kitchen anyway because he felt like a cup of tea for himself.

1092
00:53:04.410 --> 00:53:10.110
And then Pam shares her truth, which is she actually switched to coffee back in March.

1093
00:53:10.110 --> 00:53:13.510
But she still appreciates the gesture.

1094
00:53:14.390 --> 00:53:14.550
Yeah.

1095
00:53:15.130 --> 00:53:18.370
So there was a talking head here that they cut.

1096
00:53:18.910 --> 00:53:19.250
Mm-hmm.

1097
00:53:19.970 --> 00:53:22.790
Jim was going to say something like,

1098
00:53:23.810 --> 00:53:25.590
Wow, she switched to coffee.

1099
00:53:26.130 --> 00:53:28.830
And then he was going to pause and say something like,

1100
00:53:29.750 --> 00:53:32.250
But she still likes mixed berries, right?

1101
00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:35.250
Like he was going to reference the mixed berries yogurt.

1102
00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:36.210
I know.

1103
00:53:36.690 --> 00:53:37.030
Hmm.

1104
00:53:37.850 --> 00:53:41.170
You know, when I saw this scene, it really hit me.

1105
00:53:41.290 --> 00:53:43.570
I was like, this is significant.

1106
00:53:43.730 --> 00:53:46.290
You don't know her morning drink.

1107
00:53:46.410 --> 00:53:55.370
Like you are so disconnected from one another that you don't know this big piece of information that's a part of her every day.

1108
00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:56.530
You know what I mean?

1109
00:53:56.690 --> 00:53:58.410
This is the everyday intimacy.

1110
00:53:58.810 --> 00:54:01.730
And it is missing from Jim and Pam's life.

1111
00:54:01.730 --> 00:54:07.170
And I would say I think it's the very small little things in life.

1112
00:54:07.650 --> 00:54:09.890
That's how you know someone truly.

1113
00:54:10.270 --> 00:54:13.070
It's very easy to know the big things.

1114
00:54:13.250 --> 00:54:13.610
Yes.

1115
00:54:13.710 --> 00:54:18.590
You know, but it's the very small intimate things that separate a relationship.

1116
00:54:19.250 --> 00:54:19.550
Yes.

1117
00:54:20.530 --> 00:54:21.170
Exactly.

1118
00:54:21.570 --> 00:54:23.490
Well, that's just good writing, right?

1119
00:54:23.830 --> 00:54:24.210
I know.

1120
00:54:24.390 --> 00:54:25.050
It's just really good writing.

1121
00:54:25.350 --> 00:54:26.410
Really, really good writing.

1122
00:54:27.030 --> 00:54:35.370
Well, speaking of really good writing that didn't make it in, back at the laboratory, there was a scene that would have gone here.

1123
00:54:36.170 --> 00:54:41.250
And Andy, because it's an industrial shoot, had to do his own hair and makeup, Jenna.

1124
00:54:41.750 --> 00:54:42.130
Mm-hmm.

1125
00:54:42.670 --> 00:54:44.990
What does Andy know about hair and makeup?

1126
00:54:45.190 --> 00:54:47.070
He knows the world of the theater.

1127
00:54:47.550 --> 00:54:48.290
Oh, boy.

1128
00:54:48.490 --> 00:54:50.690
He does full stage makeup.

1129
00:54:50.690 --> 00:55:00.210
He, penciled in his eyebrows, he has on white eyeshadow, he has on that orange kind of base, you know, like that heavy foundation.

1130
00:55:00.530 --> 00:55:00.730
Yeah.

1131
00:55:00.970 --> 00:55:04.410
With bright red, like blushed cheeks.

1132
00:55:05.010 --> 00:55:07.530
Well, they need to see you from the back row.

1133
00:55:07.970 --> 00:55:08.770
From the balcony.

1134
00:55:09.370 --> 00:55:12.470
And you have those harsh lights on you on the theater, you know?

1135
00:55:13.410 --> 00:55:14.810
Oh, my gosh.

1136
00:55:14.950 --> 00:55:19.330
He walks into the laboratory in his lab coat with this stage makeup.

1137
00:55:19.950 --> 00:55:22.010
Everyone's reactions are hilarious.

1138
00:55:22.590 --> 00:55:24.570
And I wanted you to hear the clip.

1139
00:55:24.770 --> 00:55:25.870
It's in deleted scenes.

1140
00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:26.790
I cannot wait.

1141
00:55:27.310 --> 00:55:27.770
Ta-da!

1142
00:55:28.570 --> 00:55:30.190
Meet the lab assistant.

1143
00:55:30.890 --> 00:55:32.430
Ooh, what's with the clown face?

1144
00:55:32.630 --> 00:55:33.950
It's professional stage makeup.

1145
00:55:34.070 --> 00:55:35.910
Keeps from washing out under the harsh lights.

1146
00:55:36.170 --> 00:55:38.110
I learned it when I was Nakey Poo in the Mikado.

1147
00:55:39.070 --> 00:55:39.890
Andrew Bernard?

1148
00:55:40.290 --> 00:55:40.470
Yeah.

1149
00:55:41.390 --> 00:55:42.530
What's on your face?

1150
00:55:42.790 --> 00:55:44.610
A little of the old grease paint, governor.

1151
00:55:45.710 --> 00:55:47.150
Lashes sold separately.

1152
00:55:47.630 --> 00:55:51.090
Um, I'm going to need him to take this off immediately, okay?

1153
00:55:51.270 --> 00:55:51.370
Okay.

1154
00:55:51.490 --> 00:55:53.330
Question, is it too dark?

1155
00:55:53.350 --> 00:55:54.930
Because this is Trinidad.

1156
00:55:55.170 --> 00:55:59.310
I also have Brazilian Matador or Man of Athens.

1157
00:55:59.630 --> 00:56:00.790
How about no makeup at all?

1158
00:56:00.890 --> 00:56:01.290
No makeup.

1159
00:56:01.610 --> 00:56:02.190
No makeup.

1160
00:56:02.270 --> 00:56:02.590
Great.

1161
00:56:02.750 --> 00:56:03.290
I can do that.

1162
00:56:03.450 --> 00:56:04.210
But, question.

1163
00:56:04.450 --> 00:56:08.010
New to the area, is there a makeup remover store nearby?

1164
00:56:08.430 --> 00:56:09.750
Uh, you'll just use soap.

1165
00:56:10.550 --> 00:56:11.050
Wow.

1166
00:56:11.310 --> 00:56:11.810
Yeah.

1167
00:56:12.070 --> 00:56:12.930
I love it.

1168
00:56:13.690 --> 00:56:18.030
Halstead said, when Ed said Man of Athens, it made everyone crack up.

1169
00:56:18.710 --> 00:56:20.990
Well, I have a guest star shout out.

1170
00:56:21.290 --> 00:56:23.250
Ooh, I bet I know who it is.

1171
00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:25.190
Yes, I know you do.

1172
00:56:25.410 --> 00:56:27.110
It's the producer on set.

1173
00:56:27.350 --> 00:56:31.650
He was played by an old improv friend of mine and Warren's, Pete Holney.

1174
00:56:32.050 --> 00:56:35.270
Pete has been in so many movies and television shows.

1175
00:56:35.270 --> 00:56:38.070
You can catch him in Criminal Minds right now.

1176
00:56:38.250 --> 00:56:43.070
He was in Jury Duty by our very own Lee Eisenberg and Gene Stepnitzky.

1177
00:56:43.750 --> 00:56:46.530
He's in Monsters Season 2 on Netflix.

1178
00:56:46.750 --> 00:56:52.970
If you're a fan of the movie Elf, Pete and his real twin brother, Patrick Holney, are the

1179
00:56:52.970 --> 00:56:54.190
twin elves in the movie.

1180
00:56:54.770 --> 00:57:01.890
And Warren shared with me that it was really a really fun shoot day for him because Pete was there and they'd known each other for so long.

1181
00:57:02.090 --> 00:57:05.330
And that he was always pitching Pete for roles in the office.

1182
00:57:05.330 --> 00:57:08.090
And so he was so glad it finally worked out.

1183
00:57:08.090 --> 00:57:15.750
And I reached out to Pete and, lady, he has a really sweet story about Alison Jones, and I thought you should hear it.

1184
00:57:15.970 --> 00:57:16.450
Okay.

1185
00:57:16.950 --> 00:57:23.630
An interesting thing is, like, casting directors remember actors if they're good.

1186
00:57:24.650 --> 00:57:33.530
And when you audition for things and you don't get the part, the amazing thing is, is that the casting director's mind has to remember actors.

1187
00:57:33.530 --> 00:57:35.610
And when things come up, they remember them.

1188
00:57:35.610 --> 00:57:46.430
So Alison Jones, who is the casting director of the show, remembered me from, I don't know, auditioning the first time.

1189
00:57:46.570 --> 00:57:52.190
But the weird thing was, is that she did something wonderful to me once.

1190
00:57:52.190 --> 00:57:58.450
I was at The Grove, which is like a big shopping mall here in Los Angeles in Farmer's Market.

1191
00:57:58.690 --> 00:58:03.550
And I was going up an escalator and I just heard this voice yelling,

1192
00:58:03.550 --> 00:58:07.750
Peter, Peter, Peter Holney.

1193
00:58:08.270 --> 00:58:11.970
And I was like, who the hell is screaming my name?

1194
00:58:12.270 --> 00:58:15.950
And all these people are like looking around, seeing who the hell this Peter Holney is.

1195
00:58:16.270 --> 00:58:22.830
And I looked down and as the escalator's going up, I'm going down.

1196
00:58:22.830 --> 00:58:26.070
And I see this woman waving frantically.

1197
00:58:26.450 --> 00:58:29.350
And I looked at her like, who are you?

1198
00:58:29.630 --> 00:58:31.790
I can't even put my brain couldn't process it.

1199
00:58:32.050 --> 00:58:37.490
And she goes, it's me, Alison Jones from casting.

1200
00:58:37.490 --> 00:58:41.430
And then all of a sudden, as she was going up, I was going down.

1201
00:58:41.690 --> 00:58:43.030
She just kind of disappeared.

1202
00:58:43.870 --> 00:58:45.470
And I was just dumbstruck.

1203
00:58:45.670 --> 00:58:50.190
I didn't know what to do because I've never been cast by her yet.

1204
00:58:50.190 --> 00:58:53.530
And I was just amazed that she remembered me.

1205
00:58:54.510 --> 00:59:02.130
And I was like, oh, my God, Alison Jones knows my name, knows my face and and said my last name correctly.

1206
00:59:02.690 --> 00:59:10.910
And I just thought that was just a wonderful, I mean, all day long, I was walking around with my head up high going, all right.

1207
00:59:11.090 --> 00:59:11.790
All right, Hollywood.

1208
00:59:12.250 --> 00:59:14.230
You guys think I'm something special.

1209
00:59:16.860 --> 00:59:18.300
I love Alison.

1210
00:59:18.820 --> 00:59:19.420
I do, too.

1211
00:59:19.600 --> 00:59:21.640
Can't you just see her yelling, hey?

1212
00:59:21.860 --> 00:59:25.440
I mean, she is just a just a wonderful person.

1213
00:59:25.520 --> 00:59:29.640
And I just love that story and wanted to share that side of her with everyone that we know.

1214
00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:31.260
Yes, I love that.

1215
00:59:31.260 --> 00:59:35.400
And here's Pete's story about how this role came to be for him.

1216
00:59:35.920 --> 00:59:41.820
I was flying home to Chicago for Christmas with my kids and my lovely wife.

1217
00:59:42.000 --> 00:59:43.120
And we just landed.

1218
00:59:43.800 --> 00:59:47.080
And out of nowhere, I got a call from my agent.

1219
00:59:47.140 --> 00:59:49.960
And they said, hey, you just booked the office.

1220
00:59:50.240 --> 00:59:51.920
And I kind of laughed.

1221
00:59:52.000 --> 00:59:53.080
I go, ha, I'm in Chicago.

1222
00:59:53.080 --> 00:59:53.680
You know that.

1223
00:59:53.820 --> 00:59:54.340
I booked out.

1224
00:59:54.580 --> 00:59:57.900
Alison Jones just called up and said, she wants to book you.

1225
00:59:58.040 --> 00:59:58.940
Well, I'm in Chicago.

1226
00:59:58.940 --> 01:00:06.200
We just landed and I'm building a snowman with my daughter, Sophia, because they've never seen snow before because they live in L.A.

1227
01:00:06.660 --> 01:00:12.580
Anyways, so they were like, you need to either do this or just decline it.

1228
01:00:12.580 --> 01:00:21.680
So I thought for a minute as I put like the carrot into the snowman's face, well, I'm not going to let this opportunity pass me by.

1229
01:00:23.600 --> 01:00:30.640
I guess my Christmas vacation is going to be put on hold because I'm flying back to be on the office.

1230
01:00:31.260 --> 01:00:35.980
That day, I think I booked a flight and got there the next morning.

1231
01:00:37.220 --> 01:00:40.940
And it was just amazing because those kind of things never happen.

1232
01:00:41.180 --> 01:00:44.340
You don't just usually book a job without even auditioning for it.

1233
01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:48.260
But Alison Jones just remembered me and gave me the part.

1234
01:00:49.620 --> 01:00:53.140
Well, that is really special when you're just like, hey, you got the role.

1235
01:00:53.300 --> 01:00:53.960
You're like, what?

1236
01:00:54.440 --> 01:00:56.980
Yeah, you're like a role I didn't even know was happening.

1237
01:00:56.980 --> 01:00:58.320
It existed, yeah.

1238
01:00:58.580 --> 01:01:07.700
And Warren told me he was really happy because when you have an episode that is your episode that you wrote, you are in direct communication with the director.

1239
01:01:08.080 --> 01:01:11.860
And that doesn't normally happen when it's not your episode.

1240
01:01:11.860 --> 01:01:13.860
You don't really go to the director, right?

1241
01:01:13.980 --> 01:01:14.920
You don't pitch anything.

1242
01:01:15.100 --> 01:01:24.340
